WEBVTT

00:00:08,430 --> 00:00:20,430
We had some questions about toxicity sources when we go and take CT scans at MRI images.

00:00:20,430 --> 00:00:26,430
And is that a source of toxicity that we should be concerned about?

00:00:26,430 --> 00:00:31,430
Now, the problem with CT scans is you have a lot of radiation.

00:00:31,430 --> 00:00:38,430
The nominal CT study is equivalent to about 50 x-rays equivalent rad radiation.

00:00:38,430 --> 00:00:41,430
And of course, that's a lot of radiation.

00:00:41,430 --> 00:00:43,430
And what does radiation do with our body?

00:00:43,430 --> 00:00:45,430
Because cancer.

00:00:45,430 --> 00:00:53,430
So radiation damages cell membranes and damages the nucleus and could cause the cell to go into rapid replication.

00:00:53,430 --> 00:00:57,430
That's why people that are exposed to radiation get cancer.

00:00:57,430 --> 00:01:01,430
So what are we doing when we're going to getting CT scans every three months?

00:01:01,430 --> 00:01:04,430
Well, that's not maybe a good idea.

00:01:04,430 --> 00:01:08,430
So then the option is can I use a MRI image?

00:01:08,430 --> 00:01:12,430
Well, the MRI image, most of them you have to use a contrast.

00:01:12,430 --> 00:01:15,430
Many of the CT scans they're using a contrast too.

00:01:15,430 --> 00:01:18,430
So there's problems with those things.

00:01:18,430 --> 00:01:23,430
And I have said to you, if you said, well, how am I supposed to know I'm getting better?

00:01:23,430 --> 00:01:29,430
So the CT scan showed a 2.6 millimeter mass in my liver.

00:01:29,430 --> 00:01:31,430
How do I know if I'm getting better?

00:01:31,430 --> 00:01:33,430
I don't feel anything.

00:01:33,430 --> 00:01:41,430
Well, then again, you have to compare apples to apples if you want to know objectively that you're getting better.

00:01:41,430 --> 00:01:43,430
So you're going to have to do another CT scan.

00:01:43,430 --> 00:01:45,430
Is that the best thing to do?

00:01:45,430 --> 00:01:47,430
That's a decision you're going to have to make.

00:01:47,430 --> 00:01:50,430
Do you have to know those things?

00:01:50,430 --> 00:01:53,430
That's what's exciting about the IVG test coming out.

00:01:53,430 --> 00:01:59,430
Because we're going to have some quantitative data that we could look at and we could measure over time.

00:01:59,430 --> 00:02:01,430
And it's going to be completely not invasive.

00:02:01,430 --> 00:02:03,430
It's just a blood draw.

00:02:03,430 --> 00:02:09,430
So these are invasive tests that you've heard me say that, you know, to do an invasive test,

00:02:09,430 --> 00:02:13,430
you only do an invasive test if it's going to change the way you treat the patients.

00:02:13,430 --> 00:02:16,430
That's what I always used to tell doctors that I taught.

00:02:16,430 --> 00:02:20,430
I really do an invasive test if it's going to change the way you treat the patient.

00:02:20,430 --> 00:02:25,430
So the question that I ask you usually is that, oh, well, you want to do a CT scan.

00:02:25,430 --> 00:02:27,430
Should I do a CT scan?

00:02:27,430 --> 00:02:32,430
Well, if you did a CT scan, then you found out the cancer was worse.

00:02:32,430 --> 00:02:34,430
Would you do chemo?

00:02:34,430 --> 00:02:36,430
Would you change your treatment protocol?

00:02:36,430 --> 00:02:38,430
That's what I'm asking.

00:02:38,430 --> 00:02:40,430
If the answer is, yeah, I might.

00:02:40,430 --> 00:02:45,430
Well, then you better do the CT scan because if you're looking for that qualitative data,

00:02:45,430 --> 00:02:49,430
then you have to compare apples with apples and you have to do the test.

00:02:49,430 --> 00:02:54,430
And then you have to deal with the contrast and you have to deal with the radiation.

00:02:54,430 --> 00:02:59,430
We're going to get to a second how you could deal with those things in just a second.

00:02:59,430 --> 00:03:05,430
But these are some of the different types of contrasts that you get with CT scans.

00:03:05,430 --> 00:03:09,430
And this is some of the symptoms that you could get with the contrast.

00:03:09,430 --> 00:03:12,430
You could have an allergic sensitivity to it.

00:03:12,430 --> 00:03:15,430
And the biggest thing is a toxic sensitivity to it.

00:03:15,430 --> 00:03:21,430
You're just exposed to a toxin that now has detoxify out of your body.

00:03:21,430 --> 00:03:31,430
This is actually a slide taken to teach doctors about radiation exposure after CT scan and IV contrast reactions.

00:03:31,430 --> 00:03:34,430
And this is an antiflactic reaction pretty nasty looking.

00:03:34,430 --> 00:03:36,430
That's pretty uncommon.

00:03:36,430 --> 00:03:42,430
And that's not really what I'm really concerned with any type of an allergic reaction to an IV.

00:03:42,430 --> 00:03:46,430
I'm talking about what is it doing to a person's liver.

00:03:46,430 --> 00:03:51,430
So your body has to detoxify that not to mention the radiation.

